      "title": "User delete route lacks the admin check",
      "description": "The DELETE /users/:id route does not use requireAdmin, while its sibling routes do. The admin router has no router-level guard, and server.js mounts it without one. Any caller who can reach the server can delete any user by id. This is broken authorization on a destructive action. Add requireAdmin as middleware on the delete route, matching the other admin routes.",
      "suggested_change": "admin.delete(\"/users/:id\", requireAdmin, async (req, res) => {",
      "source": null,
      "candidate": null,
      "notes": [],
      "problem": "The DELETE /users/:id route does not use requireAdmin, while its sibling routes do. The admin router has no router-level guard, and server.js mounts it without one.",
      "consequence": "Any caller who can reach the server can delete any user by id. This is broken authorization on a destructive action.",
      "fix": "Add requireAdmin as middleware on the delete route, matching the other admin routes."
    },
    {
      "origin": "agent",
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "bug",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "file_path": "src/admin.js",
      "line_number": 22,
      "line_end": 25,
      "title": "Purge drops removeUser promises",
      "description": "The purge handler passes an async callback to forEach, so nothing awaits or catches the removeUser promises. The handler replies with ids.length before any removal finishes. removeUser throws for an unknown id, and that rejection goes unhandled, which crashes the Node process by default. The client also gets a removed count that ignores failures. Await Promise.allSettled over ids.map(db.removeUser), then report the count of fulfilled results.",
      "suggested_change": "  const results = await Promise.allSettled(ids.map((id) => db.removeUser(id)));\n  res.json({ removed: results.filter((r) => r.status === \"fulfilled\").length });",
      "source": "lens:async-floating-promise",
      "candidate": null,
      "notes": [],
      "problem": "The purge handler passes an async callback to forEach, so nothing awaits or catches the removeUser promises. The handler replies with ids.length before any removal finishes.",
      "consequence": "removeUser throws for an unknown id, and that rejection goes unhandled, which crashes the Node process by default. The client also gets a removed count that ignores failures.",
      "fix": "Await Promise.allSettled over ids.map(db.removeUser), then report the count of fulfilled results."
    },
